From: MOSS Windows lane <moss@example.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Session 0: no named-object grant

---

Upstream: anthropics/sandbox-runtime, tag v0.0.81 (c40cf14), after 0001.
Reason: a broker started by a password scheduled task (MOSS's login
item when the user isn't signed in) runs in session 0, where there is no
`\Sessions\0\BaseNamedObjects` (NTSTATUS 0xC0000034 refused every launch).
The session's named objects there are the global directory's, which a
standard user may not change and an elevated broker must not leave
changed (nothing would take the grant back).

Files: src/winsta.rs.

What changes, and nothing else: in session 0 the named-object grant is
not made and the launch goes on without it; every other session is
unchanged.

diff --git a/src/winsta.rs b/src/winsta.rs
--- a/src/winsta.rs
+++ b/src/winsta.rs
@@ -277,7 +277,20 @@
 /// session — see module doc for why there is no revoke. The broker's
 /// **user** SID has full control on the session BNO (verified by
 /// probe), so this works non-elevated.
+///
+/// Session 0 (MOSS patch 0002): a broker started by a password
+/// scheduled task runs there, where there is no `\Sessions\0\
+/// BaseNamedObjects` (the session's named objects are the global
+/// directory's). The grant is not made there at all: a standard user may
+/// not change the global directory, and an elevated broker that did would
+/// leave the sandbox group a right on it that nothing takes back. The
+/// launch goes on without it (msys2/cygwin's shared heap was measured to
+/// work there on Windows Server 2025).
 pub fn grant_sandbox_on_session_bno(sb_sid: &str) -> Result<()> {
+    if broker_session() == Some(0) {
+        eprintln!("srt-win: note: session 0: no grant on the named-object directory; launching without it");
+        return Ok(());
+    }
     let bno = open_session_bno()?;
     let si = DACL_SECURITY_INFORMATION.0;
     recompose_dacl(
@@ -317,6 +330,13 @@
     .context("grant srt-sandbox on session BaseNamedObjects")
 }
 
+/// The broker's Terminal Services session, or None when it can't be read.
+fn broker_session() -> Option<u32> {
+    let mut ts = 0u32;
+    unsafe { ProcessIdToSessionId(std::process::id(), &mut ts) }.ok()?;
+    Some(ts)
+}
+
